      "writtenOn": "2022-10-02T23:33:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What if I want 2 hours? Besides why just not make GNSS mandatery? At least it can do Stratum 0, unlike NTP, stratum field set to 0 in NTP packet indicates an unspecified stratum.",
      "revId": "fd127c62e7792ed473598b0d702b142fffbd9b33",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "60512ad7_a646a7e0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1038039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-03T09:05:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e What if I want 2 hours?\n\nDepends on your role? e.g. end user or Android device manufacturer / image developer?\n\nIf end user: You typically don\u0027t - Android\u0027s stock configuration is static. You can force refreshes by the command line in developer mode. Downstream Android device manufacturers can change this, though.\n\nIf image developer: Change the value or apply a config overlay.\n\n2 hours wouldn\u0027t achieve much. Android device clocks typically don\u0027t drift that much and we don\u0027t target high accuracy for this clock. There are other factors that limit accuracy.\n\n\u003e Besides why just not make GNSS mandatery?\n\nMost smartphones / tablets are connected to the internet most of the time. Stock Android\u0027s default NTP servers are reliable / accurate. Image developers for different form factors with different network / power profiles can change the default configuration if they wish to obtain time from other sources or change the priorities.\n\nStock Android\u0027s GNSS-based time detector logic is not enabled by default but is used on at least one form factor I\u0027m aware of without reliable network connectivity. It is passive, so relies on something else on the device getting GNSS location regularly enough (e.g. if the user runs a Map application). If the GNSS time detection client were more active (i.e. it powered up GNSS receiver hardware), it would consume power and the device would need to be able to receive GNSS signals when it does, which isn\u0027t possible in many conditions. e.g. indoors, underground.",
      "parentUuid": "3b673a1a_8903687f",
      "revId": "fd127c62e7792ed473598b0d702b142fffbd9b33",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "33c35f39_cc4e42e7",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1506910
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-03T09:42:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003eAndroid device clocks typically don\u0027t drift that much and we don\u0027t target high accuracy for this clock. There are other factors that limit accuracy.\n\nThey do drift a lot, most phones are at least 400 ms off after 24 hours, usually 1 second. In fact nanosecond stability to get GNSS fix is like 5 milliseconds only. But that is simple to check with ultra stable Javascript from https://time.is, or using NTP selectable server app (you are planning to allow setting time from apps like this, right? some new Android 13 API) https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id\u003dpartl.atomicclock\n\nor finally getting time from gnss, should be Stratum 0, most accurate https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id\u003dcom.pautinanet.smartgpstime\n\nThis is no different with windows 11, to get ms precision forever you should set 0x01 flag for *multiple* ntp servers and make w32time service automatic or it will drift like crazy, see this https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/windowsserverdocs/issues/2065#event-4029519724\n\nAre you afraid whatever NTP server you use will DDOS? I also think it would make sense to use NTP on boot, again as windows does, it has very low level kernel driver for this that does stuff on boot (leap seconds too). Reboot makes time drift very badly.\n\n\u003ewhich isn\u0027t possible in many conditions. e.g. indoors, \n\nmodern devices solve multipatch and get a 3D fix no problem near the window of if the weather is good, even not far from it.\n\nAlso about NITZ. It is no longer a default, yet you do not compare NTP and NITZ to invalidaye NITZ, do you? The point here NTP server should be close or multiple servers must be used to fix the errors. NITZ has no such problem, since it has garanteed delays.",
      "revId": "fd127c62e7792ed473598b0d702b142fffbd9b33",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
